Test-side capture of this AUDIT logger: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AuditLogCapture.java` (lines 25-79; defaults to logger name `"AUDIT"` at line 32).

---

## 4. Locality derivation

File: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java:29-30`. Locality is derived first from the postcode's first two digits, falling back to the city name, else `UNKNOWN`:
```java
@Mapping(target = "locality",
    expression = "java(switch (owner.getPostcode() != null && owner.getPostcode().matches(\"[0-9]{4}\") ? owner.getPostcode().substring(0, 2) : \"\") { case \"20\" -> \"NSW\"; case \"30\" -> \"VIC\"; case \"40\" -> \"QLD\"; default -> switch (owner.getCity()) { case \"Sydney\" -> \"NSW\"; case \"Melbourne\" -> \"VIC\"; case \"Brisbane\" -> \"QLD\"; default -> \"UNKNOWN\"; }; })")
```
OpenAPI field: `openapi.yml:2134-2135`.

Test ground-truth for locality/region: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java:227-246` (`CITY_REGION` map, `region()` helper; UNKNOWN default).

---

## 5. Region code derived from postcode

There is no standalone "region from postcode" utility in main code. The postcode→region relationship appears in two places:

- Validation (city→region postcode range check), `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/advice/OwnerFieldsValidator.java:65-82`. It rejects a 4-digit postcode outside the city's region range (Sydney 2000-2099, Melbourne 3000-3099, Brisbane 4000-4099; unknown cities accepted):
```java
int[] range = switch (owner.getCity() == null ? "" : owner.getCity()) {
    case "Sydney" -> new int[] {2000, 2099};
    case "Melbourne" -> new int[] {3000, 3099};
    case "Brisbane" -> new int[] {4000, 4099};
    default -> null;
};
int value = Integer.parseInt(postcode);
if (range != null && (value < range[0] || value > range[1])) {
    errors.rejectValue("postcode", "postcode", "must be valid for the city's region");
}
```
- Postcode-prefix→region mapping inside the `locality` derivation (OwnerMapper.java:30, item 4 above: `20`→NSW, `30`→VIC, `40`→QLD).

The `customerCode` itself does NOT currently include a region (it uses CITY3). The acceptance test expects a region-based code (item 8). Region ground truth in tests: `AcceptanceBase.java:228-237` (`CITY_REGION`, `REGION_POSTCODES`).

---

## 6. Normalized telephone handling

Primary normalization in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/advice/OwnerFieldsValidator.java:49-57`. Strips spaces/dashes/parens, assumes Australian `+61` when no `+` (dropping a leading `0`), validates via `E164Telephone`, and writes the E.164 form back onto the DTO:
```java
String raw = telephone == null ? "" : telephone.replaceAll("[\\s\\-()]", "");
String e164 = raw.startsWith("+") ? raw
    : "+61" + (raw.startsWith("0") ? raw.substring(1) : raw);
if (E164Telephone.isValid(e164)) {
    owner.setTelephone(e164);
} else {
    errors.rejectValue("telephone", "telephone", "must be a valid E.164 telephone number");
}
```

E.164 validation rules: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/advice/E164Telephone.java:29-41` (general 8-15 digits; `+61` requires 11 total digits, `+1` requires 11 total digits).

Entity constraint: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java:47-50` (`@Pattern(regexp = "^\\+[0-9]{8,15}$", message = "Phone number must be in E.164 form")`).

The normalized telephone is the primary component of the identity key: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/IdentityKey.java:19-21` (`normalizedTelephone|email|householdId`), used for duplicate detection in `IdentityKey.isDuplicate` (lines 24-27), called at `OwnerRestControllerV1.java:109`.

---

## 7. SHA-256 / hashing utilities

Only one place: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/HouseholdDuplicates.java` (imports at lines 3-4; `householdId` at 45-57). It computes a SHA-256 over a normalized `lastName\naddress` key and takes the first 8 bytes as a 16-hex-char household id:
```java
private static String householdId(String key) {
    try {
        byte[] digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256").digest(key.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
            sb.append(String.format("%02x", digest[i]));
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
    catch (Exception e) { throw new IllegalStateException(e); }
}
private static String key(Owner owner) {
    return norm(owner.getLastName()) + "\n" + norm(owner.getAddress());
}
```
`assignHousehold` (lines 35-43) sets this hash-derived id and household size; called at `OwnerRestControllerV1.java:108`. There is no general-purpose hashing utility class elsewhere (grep for `MessageDigest`/`SHA` returns only this file).

---

## 8. Tests covering these behaviors

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java:10-15` — the one active assertion on customerCode. Its docstring (line 7) states the intended redesign, and it asserts a format the current code does NOT produce:
```java
// global-id: Redesign owner identity. The customerCode must become '<REGION>-<HASH8>' ...
assertTrue(n.get("customerCode").asText().matches("[A-Z0-9]+-[0-9A-F]{8}")); // <REGION>-<HASH8>
```
This expects `<REGION>-<HASH8>` (uppercase hex), whereas `CustomerCodes.build` currently emits `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`. So the acceptance test currently fails against shipped code — this is the redesign target.

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` — MockMvc harness and pinned ground truth. Relevant: owner payload builders `ownerNode()` (117-125), `withPostcode()` (129-132), `knownOwner()` (258-265); `createOwnerOk`/`fetchOwner` (160-204); region/postcode/timezone lookups and `region()` helper (221-256).

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AuditLogCapture.java` — utility to assert AUDIT-logger side effects (item 3); not yet exercised by a customerCode test.

No dedicated unit tests exist for `CustomerCodes`, `CheckDigit`, `IdentityKey`, `HouseholdDuplicates`, `OwnerFieldsValidator`, `E164Telephone`, or `OwnerMapper`; coverage of these behaviors is only through the acceptance layer.

